      "id": 1,
      "text": "Woodbine earned its name from the abundant woodbine plant that grew in local fields and along the riverbanks.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodbine%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "During the Civil War, Confederate cavalry crossed the Patapsco River at Woodbine, scouting Union troops en route to Gettysburg.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://alchetron.com/Woodbine%2C-Maryland",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the 1920s and 1930s, Woodbine hosted a large canning factory on the Carroll County side of the Patapsco River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodbine%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A small water‑powered canning plant at New Port Hill later became the Woodbine Canning Factory, burned in 1933, and was converted to a paper mill in the 1950s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodbine%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Woodbine sits at the junction of the Patapsco River, the B&O Railroad, and Liberty Road (MD Route 26) leading north to Westminster.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodbine%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
